newCallBindT -- SEQ 510 (Tony, 2026-11-02): every new-road call instruct took the callee of the LAST call built. INVOKEr
returned its own local `instruct` by identity, so every built call was one node (measured: addrOf, same field and body on
every build). PRE-REGISTERED ON TRUNK, born red.

One process, ON PURPOSE: the defect is cross-talk between builds, so the rows must share one. Every action comes in a
pair with the same body: n built on the NEW road (nlNew), o on the OLD road (nlOld), the control. The callees pc (prints
9), pd (8) and pz (7) are plain actions, not under test. Bodies are indented, never braced (fixit braceInCode). pop.sh
asserts no `parse failed` (a failed new-road build falls back to the old road silently, CC-6) and reads each block from its
`CB <row> <road>` line.

BUILD ORDER IS PART OF THE TEST: c2 (self-call), then c1a / c1b, then c1B / c1A (the same two callees, built the other
way round), c3, c4, and c5 LAST -- c5 calls pz, so on trunk every new-road call runs pz and prints 7.

ROWS
BUILD nothing prints while the actions are built. On trunk the calls RUN AT BUILD (9 8 8 9 9 8 9 7) -- the second face of
      the one shared node (SEQ 509 saw it as an assignment call running at build).
RP    road proof: new [ 7 ], old [     7 ].
CB-1  two actions each calling a different callee, built in either order: c1a 9, c1b 8, c1B 8, c1A 9.
CB-2  a self-call built BEFORE unrelated calls (SEQ 509's reproduction): a declared counter (2) counts down: 0 1 2.
CB-3  one body making two different calls: 9 8.
CB-4  a call inside a loop body, three passes, the action run twice: 9 9 9 9 9 9.
CB-5  the last call built: 7 (the control that the cure does not break the call it used to keep).
AL-3 (newLocalT) stays pinned as it is: it passes only because nothing with a call is built after it -- its masker.
